            <div xml:id="d126">

                <pb facs="0203" n="121" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg121"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 811.34537/56.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Habana,
                                Cuba</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1911-04-17"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">April 17, 1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 758.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the project to enlarge
                    our naval station at Guantánamo, I have the honor to transmit herewith a copy of
                    a, “Confidential memorandum” which I read to Sr. Sanguily, the Cuban secretary
                    of state, this afternoon, and left with him at his request. This memorandum was
                    compiled from several of the Department’s instructions or their accompanying
                    documents (I from No. 254,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d126-f1"/> II from No. 278, and
                    III from No. 277), and was presented to Sr. Sanguily as a reply to his
                    confidential note of February 8, of which a copy was sent in my despatch No.
                    623.</p>
                <p>With regard to the progress of the negotiations, Sr. Sanguily said that the
                    President and Government are ready to make the desired exchange of additional
                    land at Guantánamo in return for the Bahía Honda release, and that the draft of
                    a treaty in the premises is being prepared in consultation with the members of
                    the senate committee on foreign relations. Some time ago a commission was sent
                    to Guantánamo, but its report was unsatisfactory, and a new technical commission
                    has now been named with instructions to study the limits of the proposed new
                    concession on the spot, and to report upon the ownership and value of the land
                    included therein and upon other pertinent matters.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John B. Jackson.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d126-a1.xml" xpointer="d126-a1"/>
            </div>
